# Template only -- lists the variable names this stack's docker-compose.yml
# expects. Real values live in Infisical, never here and never in git.
# scripts/render-env.sh writes the actual .env next to this file on the host.
#
# FLAGGED MISMATCH (found while writing this comment pass): docker-compose.yml
# actually references ${POSTGRES_USER} and ${POSTGRES_DB} directly (in the
# db service's healthcheck and the postgresql-backup service), so those two
# need real values in .env -- they were missing from this file entirely
# before this pass. Added below. Separately, the backend/redis/db/backup
# services all use `env_file: .env`, meaning whatever else you put in this
# file also gets passed into those containers wholesale as environment
# variables -- so this is also where Infisical's own app-level config
# (e.g. its ENCRYPTION_KEY, AUTH_SECRET, SITE_URL, REDIS_URL, and similar
# settings from Infisical's own self-hosting docs) would go, even though
# docker-compose.yml itself doesn't reference those by name with ${...}
# syntax. This file doesn't attempt to enumerate all of those -- check
# Infisical's self-hosting docs for the current full list your version
# needs, and add them here as you go.

# Postgres role/database name for Infisical's own dedicated `db` container.
# Used directly by docker-compose.yml (the db healthcheck, and the
# postgresql-backup sidecar's POSTGRES_USER/POSTGRES_DB). The corresponding
# password is NOT set here -- see the secrets file note below.
POSTGRES_USER=REPLACE_ME
POSTGRES_DB=REPLACE_ME

# --- File-based Docker secret ---
# Unlike most stacks in this repo, Infisical's own Postgres password isn't
# a plain env var: docker-compose.yml's top-level `secrets:` block wires
# ./secrets/infisical_db_pw.txt in as POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE for both the
# db container and the postgresql-backup sidecar. Create that one file by
# hand with a long random password before first start:
#
#   secrets/infisical_db_pw.txt
#
# That file (and the rest of secrets/) is covered by the repo's
# .gitignore and is never committed. Note: only infisical_db_pw.txt is
# actually referenced anywhere in this stack's docker-compose.yml today --
# an earlier version of this comment mentioned additional secrets files
# (infisical_pg_user.txt, infisical_encryption_key.txt,
# infisical_auth_secret.txt, infisical_db_uri.txt) that aren't wired into
# the compose file's `secrets:` block at all, so they don't do anything as
# things stand. If Infisical's own app config (ENCRYPTION_KEY, AUTH_SECRET,
# etc. -- see the note above) should also move to file-based secrets
# eventually, that's a real follow-up worth doing, but it isn't done yet.
